---
import PlaygroundShell from '../../components/PlaygroundShell.astro';
import RangeField from '../../components/RangeField.astro';
---
<PlaygroundShell title="Back-of-the-envelope numbers" hint="How much of the key space each node owns, how many copies can be down while reads and writes still work, and how much a sloppy quorum helps when nodes fail. A teaching model with round numbers, not Amazon's data.">
  <div class="pg est" data-est>
    <div class="pg-row">
      <RangeField label="Nodes in the ring" name="n" min="10" max="300" step="10" value="100" />
      <RangeField label="Copies of each key (N)" name="c" min="1" max="5" value="3" />
      <RangeField label="Replies a read waits for (R)" name="r" min="1" max="5" value="2" />
      <RangeField label="Acknowledgements a write waits for (W)" name="w" min="1" max="5" value="2" />
      <RangeField label="Chance that any one node is down (%)" name="f" min="0" max="20" step="1" value="5" />
    </div>
    <dl class="est-out" data-out aria-live="polite"></dl>
    <p class="pg-note">Teaching model, not Amazon's system or data. The paper says the common setting is (N, R, W) = (3, 2, 2) and that an instance ran a couple of hundred nodes. The failure chance is my guess that you can change. Assumptions: nodes fail independently, keys and tokens are spread evenly, a strict quorum needs W (or R) of the N owners up, and a sloppy quorum needs W healthy nodes anywhere in the ring, because it can use the next healthy node on the ring. R and W above N are treated as N. Real failures are often correlated, for example a whole rack or data center, which is why the paper spreads copies across data centers.</p>
  </div>
</PlaygroundShell>
<script>
  import { query, numberValue, formatOneDecimalBelow100 } from '../../playground';
  document.querySelectorAll<HTMLElement>('[data-est]').forEach((root) => {
    const q = query(root);
    const val = numberValue(q);
    const OUT_ROWS = 11; // lines in the result list
    const rows = Array.from({ length: OUT_ROWS }, () => { const dt = document.createElement('dt'); const dd = document.createElement('dd'); q('[data-out]').append(dt, dd); return { dt, dd }; });
    const f1 = formatOneDecimalBelow100;
    const pct = (n: number) => `${(Math.round(n * 1000) / 1000).toLocaleString('en-US')}%`;
    function draw() {
      ['n', 'c', 'r', 'w', 'f'].forEach((key) => (q(`[data-${key}val]`).textContent = String(val(`[data-${key}]`))));
      const nodes = val('[data-n]'); const copies = val('[data-c]');
      const rr = Math.min(val('[data-r]'), copies); const ww = Math.min(val('[data-w]'), copies);
      const fail = val('[data-f]') / 100; const up = 1 - fail;
      const choose = (n: number, k: number) => { let out = 1; for (let i = 1; i <= k; i++) out = (out * (n - k + i)) / i; return out; };
      const atLeast = (n: number, k: number, p: number) => { let sum = 0; for (let i = k; i <= n; i++) sum += choose(n, i) * p ** i * (1 - p) ** (n - i); return Math.min(1, sum); };
      const data: [string, string][] = [
        ['Share of the key space each node owns', pct(100 / nodes)],
        ['Share of all keys each node stores a copy of', pct((100 * copies) / nodes)],
        ['Do the read and write sets overlap (R + W > N)?', rr + ww > copies ? `yes (${rr} + ${ww} > ${copies})` : `no (${rr} + ${ww} <= ${copies})`],
        ['Owners that can be down while writes still work', String(copies - ww)],
        ['Owners that can be down while reads still work', String(copies - rr)],
        ['Chance at least one owner of a key is down, so a hint is needed', pct(100 * (1 - up ** copies))],
        ['Strict quorum: chance a write succeeds', pct(100 * atLeast(copies, ww, up))],
        ['Strict quorum: chance a read succeeds', pct(100 * atLeast(copies, rr, up))],
        ['Sloppy quorum: chance a write succeeds', pct(100 * atLeast(nodes, ww, up))],
        ['Chance all copies of a key are down at once', pct(100 * fail ** copies)],
        ['Extra load on each remaining node when one node fails', pct(100 / (nodes - 1))],
      ];
      rows.forEach((r, i) => { r.dt.textContent = data[i][0]; r.dd.textContent = data[i][1]; });
    }
    root.addEventListener('input', draw);
    draw();
  });
</script>
